    "title": "Warrants issued for men charged in alleged $200K NRG Stadium parking scam - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Catherine Dominguez ,\n1 days ago Fans make their way to NRG Stadium before a football game Thursday, Nov. 20, 2025. (Brett Coomer/Houston Chronicle) Warrants have been issued for two men charged in an alleged parking lot scam at Houston's NRG Stadium, where they and another man used personal credit card readers and PayPal to charge over 7,000 unsuspecting drivers for parking.\nTaelor Miller, Vincent Ward and Reginald Smith are facing felony charges after Houston Police Department investigators say they embezzled over $200,000 from drivers dating back to March 2024, according to Harris County court documents filed Friday.\nMORE ON HOUSTONCHRONICLE.COM: Harris County Tax Office worker avoids prison after bribery plea in car title scheme\nOne victim told detectives he and several of his friends attended a Texans football game on Aug. 28, 2024. However, when they pulled into Gate 3 on Holly Hall Drive, he was charged $50 by a parking lot attendant. When the man tried to park, he was directed out of the lot, which was designated for pre-paid parking, according to documents.\nThe man spoke with the parking lot manager, who said she had problems with the attendant and was waiting for security to approach him regarding the attendant's use of Square and PayPal to collect money from drivers instead of the company's Park Hub account.\nInvestigators found Square records showing that Ward allegedly had 4,021 successful credit card swipes netting $122,818.95, Miller had 2,100 swipes netting $ 56,790.79, and Smith had 1,041 swipes netting $ 25,192.09.\nNRG officials declined to comment on the issue.\nSign up for Houston Chronicle newsletters here\nRead the original article on houstonchronicle.com\n22",
